🚽 tune.brainrot — every tuning constant in the game, in one place.
🚽
🚽 Brainrot has no top-level globals and no preprocessor constants, so the
🚽 single source of truth is a set of zero-argument accessor functions. They
🚽 are cooked into both the game and the tests, which is what keeps the
🚽 golden files honest: change a number here and the tests move with it.
🚽
🚽 `skibidi main` caches these into `deadass` locals once at startup, so the
🚽 per-frame cost is zero. See DESIGN.md section 5.

🚽 --- screen ---
rizz t_screen_w()        { bussin 1280; }
rizz t_screen_h()        { bussin 720; }
chad t_ground_y()        { bussin 560.0; }

🚽 --- player ---
chad t_player_x()        { bussin 200.0; }
chad t_player_w()        { bussin 48.0; }
chad t_player_h()        { bussin 96.0; }
chad t_gravity()         { bussin 2600.0; }
chad t_jump_v()          { bussin -1000.0; }
rizz t_hearts_max()      { bussin 3; }
chad t_iframe_time()     { bussin 1.2; }
chad t_hit_slowdown()    { bussin 0.75; }

🚽 --- bat ---
chad t_attack_total()    { bussin 0.45; }
chad t_attack_active()   { bussin 0.30; }
chad t_hitbox_dx()       { bussin 40.0; }
chad t_hitbox_dy()       { bussin 16.0; }
chad t_hitbox_w()        { bussin 56.0; }
chad t_hitbox_h()        { bussin 56.0; }

🚽 --- world ---
chad t_speed_start()     { bussin 260.0; }
chad t_speed_accel()     { bussin 3.0; }
chad t_speed_max()       { bussin 900.0; }
chad t_enemy_close()     { bussin 40.0; }
chad t_dt_max()          { bussin 0.05; }
chad t_sahur_distance()  { bussin 200000.0; }   🚽 M3

🚽 --- spawning ---
chad t_spawn_x()         { bussin 1340.0; }
chad t_gap_base()        { bussin 1.55; }
chad t_gap_slope()       { bussin 0.0011; }
chad t_gap_min()         { bussin 0.55; }
chad t_gap_jitter()      { bussin 0.20; }
chad t_fair_jump_jump()  { bussin 0.95; }
chad t_fair_any()        { bussin 0.60; }

🚽 --- pools ---
rizz t_max_obstacles()   { bussin 16; }
rizz t_max_enemies()     { bussin 16; }

🚽 --- sprites ---
🚽 One frame of the Tung atlases, and the column its torso sits on. The art
🚽 is wider than the 48px collision box on purpose -- the trailing leg and
🚽 the bat reach past it -- so the draw call offsets by the anchor rather
🚽 than using the player's x directly. tools/process_sprites.py prints these
🚽 when it builds the atlases; they are not free parameters.
rizz t_sprite_w()        { bussin 103; }
rizz t_sprite_h()        { bussin 96; }
rizz t_sprite_anchor()   { bussin 45; }
rizz t_run_frames()      { bussin 6; }
rizz t_swing_frames()    { bussin 3; }
rizz t_patapim_frames()  { bussin 5; }

🚽 Pixels of ground per Patapim stride. Shorter than Tung's: it is a
🚽 four-legged bound, and it closes faster than the world scrolls.
chad t_patapim_stride()  { bussin 26.0; }

🚽 Pixels of ground covered per run frame. Distance-driven rather than
🚽 timed, so the feet stay in step with the scroll at every speed instead
🚽 of skating once the world outruns a fixed frame rate.
chad t_run_stride()      { bussin 22.0; }

🚽 --- entity kinds ---
🚽 0 crate  48x48 grounded  -> jump
🚽 1 post   40x96 grounded  -> jump
🚽 2 brr brr patapim 64x64  -> bonk
rizz t_kind_crate()      { bussin 0; }
rizz t_kind_post()       { bussin 1; }
rizz t_kind_patapim()    { bussin 2; }

🚽 --- run states ---
rizz t_state_title()     { bussin 0; }
rizz t_state_run()       { bussin 1; }
rizz t_state_over()      { bussin 2; }

🚽 --- keys (raylib keycodes; see docs/brainray.md) ---
rizz t_key_space()       { bussin 32; }
rizz t_key_up()          { bussin 265; }
rizz t_key_x()           { bussin 88; }
rizz t_key_z()           { bussin 90; }
rizz t_key_r()           { bussin 82; }
